    "number": 36576,
    "state": "open",
    "state_reason": null,
    "title": "bip152: Should we be disconnecting peers for blocks with \"null\" tx?",
    "body": "In `InitData`, we run checks against prefilled txns to see if the tx are `IsNull`, which means zero inputs and outputs. If this check fails, we mark the attempt as `READ_STATUS_INVALID`, then disconnect the peer.\n\nThis seems contrary to BIP152 which is here: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0152.mediawiki#cmpctblock . The checks listed in the BIP don't seem to cover this or are at last unclear: \"A node MUST NOT send a cmpctblock message without having validated that the header properly commits to each transaction in the block, and properly builds on top of the existing, fully-validated chain with a valid proof-of-work either as a part of the current most-work valid chain, or building directly on top of it. A node MAY send a cmpctblock before validating that each transaction in the block validly spends existing UTXO set entries.\"\n\nSimilarly, it's not entirely clear if we should be disconnecting due to completely empty blocks, which bip152 doesn't seem to suggest.\n\n**Core doesn't send either of these, because it runs `CheckBlock` before forwarding.**\n\nOpening an issue so this can be documented at least, because changing our behavior to not disconnect could result in us asking for full blocks we definitely wouldn't want anyways.\n\nPondering from reviewing https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35321 which extends misbehavior to the unprompted case as well.",
